89
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Well, this week I have email questions to answer.

90
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
I have one, two, three, four of them,

91
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
and that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

92
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
So let's get started. shall we

93
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:53,000
E-mail? that is, folks,

94
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
And here's an answer to one of your e-mails.

95
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
We're going to begin with an e-mail

96
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
in which I will hold the sender anonymous

97
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
and the reason will be clear as soon as I get started here.

98
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
An issue surfaced in my troop

99
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
and it's explained in this e-mail I received from a parent.

100
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Our son has started counseling.

101
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
His teachers recommended this

102
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
because they're worried about his anxiety levels.

103
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
They've also recommended he needed additional help

104
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
with a couple of other things.

105
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
So he already feels a little bit like

106
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
the world is conspiring against him.

107
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
He's having an issue with scouts.

108
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
He's very offended by the language

109
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
used by some of the scouts in the troop.

110
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
To be fair, it probably isn't anything

111
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
he hasn't heard coming out of my mouth

112
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
on many occasions, but coming from his peers

113
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
it's disturbing him.

114
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
He's also feeling a little ostracized

115
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
and left out because he doesn't join in

116
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
with the bad language and therefore

117
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
he's on the outside looking in.

118
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
I think the main thing is that he said

119
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
that because of this

120
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
this is no longer fun anymore.

121
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
He's talked about changing troops

122
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
and I'm not sure that would be the answer.

123
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
I can't say one troop would be better

124
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
at suppressing language than the other.

125
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
I know what I heard when I was a scout.

126
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
When my wife asked me if I ever felt ostracized

127
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
by my Boy Scout troop,

128
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
I replied with a resounding yes.

129
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
So that was the message from the parent,

130
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
and the person who sent me this question

131
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
went on to say:

132
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
should the conference be helpful?

133
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Should adults be more involved

134
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
in disciplining inappropriate behavior,

135
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
even if we don't witness it?

136
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Are we letting the inmates run the asylum?

137
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
I'm at a loss right now and I could use some advice.

138
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
If you have any thoughts,

139
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
I would appreciate you sharing them.

140
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
I'm concerned about the use of foul language

141
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
in my troop, but I'm not sure to the extent of it.

142
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
I'm not sure about the anxiety issue.

143
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Perhaps this scout is a special case.

144
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
We've been very successful

145
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
in having a youth-led experience

146
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
and the scouting question is a fairly new scout

147
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
who joined last spring.

148
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Well, let's see if we can sort this out a little bit.

149
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Most of the time, if one scout

150
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
is complaining about the behavior of other scouts

151
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
and nobody else seems to be bothered about it,

152
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
then the likelihood that it has more to do with the scout

153
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
or the parent who's complaining

154
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
than the seriousness of the problem.

155
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Now, naturally,

156
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
I would never say that foul language is good,

157
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
but it's almost always not

158
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
an absolute serious problem.

159
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
You know, while I'm saying that

160
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
it's not often a serious problem,

161
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
it's a problem and it should be addressed-

162
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
and I should be addressed directly

163
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and I would make it the subject of a scout master minute.

164
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
I would talk to my patrol leader's counsel about it.

165
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
I would say that this is a concern

166
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
that we need to deal with

167
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and you guys need to work on

168
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
your observance of the Scout Oath and Law

169
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
as far as these matters are concerned.

170
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
I wouldn't necessarily go about

171
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
dictating this to them

172
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
as much as encouraging them

173
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
to think about it and to empathize

174
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
with people who might be offended by that kind of language

175
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and whether or not they think that is proper

176
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
in the context of what they're doing as scouts.

177
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Get them thinking, Get them working on it,

178
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Get them empathizing with one another,

179
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Get them thinking about the Scout Oath and Law.

180
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
It's actually a very good opportunity

181
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
to do just that

182
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
and that's the way we work with

183
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
these types of issues in a scout troop.

184
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
The scout you're talking about

185
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
sounds very familiar to me.

186
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
He's a little fragile, He's a little frightened,

187
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
He's younger, He's looking for some reassurance

188
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
and for some reason the foul language

189
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
is something he's perseverating on.

190
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
but this may be due to the other anxieties

191
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
that he's experiencing,

192
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
and it can get very complicated

193
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
If he doesn't make friends easily

194
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and he has feelings of ostracism.

195
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
he's finding things difficult

196
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Now. this won't last forever.

197
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
I found this is a pretty common thing

198
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
that happened with boys.

199
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
They feel like outsiders

200
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
and that kind of fuels their fears

201
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
of being ostracized

202
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
and they get defensive and sensitive

203
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
to lots of things

204
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
and then their peers react to that

205
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
and that reaction supports their fears

206
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
and the cycle just kind of keeps on going.

207
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
but, like I said, happily

208
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
a lot of this fades as they get a little bit older.

209
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Now it can make parents

210
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
absolutely crazy, and I understand,

211
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
because your child is in distress

212
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
and they're trying to figure out

213
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
what the heck is going on.

214
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
and between the ages of 11 and 14,

215
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
most of the scouts that I have worked with

216
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
are going to have done this,

217
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
what I call going on the dark side

218
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
of the moon- for a while.

219
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
It could be a number of months,

220
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
It could be a year or so,

221
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
but they just seem to be out of the range of communication.

222
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
They're in their own chaotic world,

223
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
blindly pushing buttons,

224
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
lashing out at things,

225
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
trying to figure things out,

226
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
and that can make them very anxious,

227
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
it can make them kind of surly and mean,

228
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
it can make them emotional and unpredictable.

229
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
they act out in all kinds of ways,

230
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and some of them can be dangerous

231
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and negative, and some of them can be

232
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
like what you're experiencing,

233
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
with just a lot of anxiety

234
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
and feelings of ostracism involved.

235
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
they're looking for consistency

236
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
and constancy in the world.

237
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
they're figuring out how the world works.

238
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
now, if the adults get all reactive

239
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
to the anxiety

240
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
or to the bad behavior

241
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
or they try to affect those behaviors

242
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
with a change in circumstances,

243
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
things are going to become even more unsettled.

244
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:46,000
if parents and scouts will hold the line,

245
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
stick to their word

246
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
and remain as consistent as possible,

247
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
you're showing your sons

248
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and your scouts

249
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
that there is some peace and order

250
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
in at least some aspect of their lives,

251
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
that they can step out of that

252
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
chaotic life that they're experiencing

253
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
onto some solid ground.

254
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
they won't understand this very much,

255
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
but they will ultimately appreciate it.

256
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
so what does a scouter

257
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
or a parent do?

258
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
well, as a parent,

259
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
I'd pretty much require some

260
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
highly interactive social activity

261
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
like scouts right.

262
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
if the child fights me on that-

